Law 48: Assume Formlessness

Transgression of the Law: Sparta and Athens always fought each other. Sparta never changed it's ways so even though Athens was defeated in war, the Athenians adapted and used money to creep into Spartan ways. 

Interpretation of the law: People wieghed down by a system or an inflexible way of doing things cannot move fast, cannot sense or adapt to change. Learn to move fast and adapt or you will be eaten. 

Observance of the Law: Chiang Kai-shek was defeated by Mao Tse-tung because he would slowly divide the army (Chiang was a student of Carl Von Clausewitz). Finally, the nationalist armies were defeated by the communists after they were divided and surrounded. The morale also broken. This was more impressive because Chiang was also technically superior. 

Interpretation: Mao was fond of the game Go or in China as it's called Wei-chi. The game is much more fluid than it's Wester counterpart Chess. The goal is different as well, but Mao took much inspiration from the game during these battles. 

A key Wei-chi concept is to use the size of the board to your advantage, spreading out in every direction so that your opponent cannot fathom your movements in a simple linear way. 

Do not give your opponents anything solid to attack; your enemy will exhaust themselves trying to cope with your elusiveness. 

Keys to Power: 

Power can only thrive if it is flexible in its form. To be formless is not to be amorphous; everything has a form- it is impossible to avoid. 

T.E. Lawrence fought the turks and blended into the dessert to allude the enemy. He also influenced Mao. 

The first psychological requirement of formlessness is to train yourself to take nothing personally. Be like a slippery ball that cannot be held: Let noone know what gets to you, or where your weaknesses lie. Make your face a formless mask and you will infuriate and disorient your scheming colleagues and opponents. A good example of this is Baron James Rothschild who adapted to everything. 

Female leaders had it the toughest, they couldn't attach themselves to ideological struggles for fear of appearing too emotional but also couldn't repress their emotions because they would receive criticism as well. 

One of the best female leaders was Queen Elizabeth of England. She kept her fluidity through the war between protestants and catholics. 

Another female leader whose fluidity was her advantage was Russia's Catherine the great, who understood Russia's moods. 

When you find yourself in conflict with someone stronger and more rigid, allow them a momentary victory. Seem to bow to their superiority. 
Then. by being formless and adaptable slowly insuinate yourself into your soul. This way you will catch them off guard, for rigid people are always ready to ward off direct blows but are helpless against the subtle and insinuating. 

The unintelligent are often seduced into believing that size connotes power, the bigger the better. 

Xerxes lost against Greece because: The flexible and fleet of foot will almost always win, for they have more strategic options. The more gigantic the enemy, the easier it is to induce a collapse. 

The need for formlessness becomes greater the older we get, as we grow more likely to become set in our ways and assume too rigid of a form. We become predictable, always the first sign of decreptitude. 

An enemy who doesn't respect you will grow bold, and boldness makes even the smallest animal dangerous. 

Marie Antionette was a good example of this, sticking to the old ways until she was executed. 

As you get older, you must rely even less on your past. 

Never forget, though, that formlessness is a stragetic pose. 

The laws that govern circumstance are abolished by new circumstance. 

Reversal: When you play with formlessness, keep on top of the process, and keep your long term strategy in mind. When you assume a form and go on the attack, use concentration, speed, and power. 

 

On Sun, Nov 1, 2026 at 1:59 AM isaac marinero <arnoldomarinero@gmail.com> wrote:
Law 47: Do Not Go Past the Mark You Aimed For; In Victory, Learn When to Stop

Trangession of the law: 559 B.C. Cyrus of Persia fought and won many wars for Persia. Finally, he wanted to battle Massegatai. Cyrus took the son of the queen hostage, and the son commited suicide from the humilation. The queen warned Cyrus to retreat but he refused and died in battle. 

Interpretation: His many victories had gone to his head, clouding his judgement. Instead of recognzing each situation as different, he thought each new war would bring the same result as the one before as long as he used the methods he knew: ruthless force and cunnin. 

Observance of the Law: Madame de Pompadour took the King Louis XV even though was of a lower class. Many people resented her for that but she was kind and helpful to everybody. 

Interpretation of the Law: She wasn't like the other mistresses who became greedy as soon as they saw their loss of interest from the king. Instead of bullying the courtiers of the court, she gained their support. When she couldn't peform her duties in bed anymore, she suggested a younger woman because she knew the king would bore with them. Success plays tricks on your mind. It makes you feel invulnerable, while also making you feel more hostile and emotional when people challenge your power. 

Keys to Power: Power has it's own rhythms and patterns. The lesson is simple: The powerful vary their rhythms and patterns, change course, adapt to cirumstance, and learn to improvice. Steady yourself after a victory, and give yourself the space to reflect what has happened, examine the role of circumstance and luck in success. Bad luck teaches valuable lessons about patience, timing, and the need to be prepared for the worst; good luck deludes you into the oppposite lesson. 

Pericles led a small of handful victories. He warned the Athenians not to push into other countries. The demagogues took over not heeding the warning and ended up getting destroyed. When you are victorious, then lie low, and lull the enemy into inaction. Wu Ch'i instead beheaded his officer because he was talented but disobedient. 

As lawyers say with cross examination, "Stop with a victory". 

Reversal: As Machiavell says, either destroy a man or leave him alone entirely. 





