Law 46: Never Appear Too Perfect 

Trangression of the Law: Halliwell was envious of Orton so he killed him for his success. 

Interpretation: Orton's attempts to help launch Halliwell's career were registered as charity and guilt. Orton basically had two possible solutions to the problem which were to downplay his own success, displaying some faults to deflect the envy or realized the nature of the problem and fled. Once success happens your way, however, the people to fear the most are those in your own circle, the friends, acquitantances you have left behind. 

Observance of the Law: Cosimo was a modest individual externally even though internally, he had much wealth. 

Never be so foolosh as to believe that you are stirring up adminiration by flauting the qualities that raise you above others. 

Keys to Power: Politcal power of any kind creates envy, and one of the best ways to deflect it before it takes root is to seem unambitious. Upon occasion, reveal a harmless defect in your character. 

Reversal: Bramante beat out Michelangelo to work on the Pope's tomb. Michelangelo instead took the offer to make the Sistine Chapel which ended up being one of his best works. Every time Bramante heard of it or saw it , he felt more oppressed by his own envy- the sweetest and most lasting revenge you can exact on the envious. 


