Law 45: Preach the Need for Change, but Never reform too much at once

Trangression of the Law: Cromwell convince King Henry VIII to become protestant and the change happened too quickly. The people didn't like their old ways ripped from so quickly. 
The last nail in the coffin was a painting of a protestant German princess that exaggerated her looks to try to get the King to marry her. 

Intepretation: The man who intiates strong reforms often becomes the scapegoat for any kind of dissatisfaction. 

Observance of the Law: Mao from China. He appealed to ancient stories to further communism. 

Chinese books to look into: The Water Margin (Chinese Robinhood) 
                                           The Romance of the Three Kings 
                                            Not a book, but looking into Great Emperor Chi'n

Mao's main opponent was Lin Paio. Mao's philosophy was legalism exemplified by the writing of Han-fei-tzu. 

Intepretation: The lesson is simple: the past is powerful. When you destroy the familiar you create a void or vacuum, people fear the chaos that will flood in to fill it. 

Keys to Power: Pay attention to the zeitgiest, because if the reform is too far ahead of it's time few will understand it and it will stir up anxiety and be hopelessly misinterpreted. 

Watch the Zeitgiest. The opposite is true and if you work in a tulmultous time there is power to be gained by preaching a return to the past, to comfort, tradition and ritual. 

Reversal: The past is a corpse to use as you see fit. If what happened in the recent past was painful and harsh, it is self destructive to associate yourself with it. 

