Law 44: Disarn and Infuriate: 

Mirror Effects: Preliminary Typology
 The effect contains great power because it operates on the most primitive emotions.

The are four main Mirror Effects in the realm of Power: 
 
The Nuetralizing effect: Do what your enemies do, following their actions as best you can. This is the warrior's mirror. A reverse version of the Neutralizing Effect is the shadow 

The Narcissus Effect: This is simply the ability to mimic another person not physically, but psychologically, and it is immensely powerful because it plays upon the unsatisfied self-love of a child. Thr Narcissus effect works wonders in both social life and business; it gives us both the Seducer's and Courtier's Mirror 

The Moral Effect: In the moral Effect, you mirror what other people have done to you, and do so in a way that makes them realize you are doing to them exactly what they did to you. This is the teacher's mirror. 

The Hallucinatory Effect: The Hallucinatory effect comes from creating a perfect copy of an object, a place, a person. This is the deceiver's mirror. 

Oberservance I: Fouche, Napoleon's King of Police. Fouche stayed one step ahead of Napoleon by also keeping spies on Napoleon like Napoleon kept spies on Fouche. 

Observance II: General Alcibiades, orated well and mirrored the desires of Athenians, but when he lost their goodwill moved to Sparta where he mirrored their livestyle and finally mirrored the Pesians while secretly helping the Athenians. He used the seducer's mirror but went too far.

Oberservance III: Marie Mancini & King Louis XIV

Interpretation: She used the Seducer's mirror on King Louis XIV

Observance IV: Ivan the Terrible placed a king's double to teach the Boyars who defied him a lesson. 

Intepretation: When you mirror someone's behavior, adding a touch of a caricature and exaggeration, as Ivan did by enthroning a Tartar - it is the little spice in the soup that can open people's eyes and make them see the ridiculousness in their own actiosn. 

Observance V: Dr. Milton Erickson guided their patients using this technique. He asked a patient who belived he was Jesus to become a carpenter like Jesus was. This helped the patient integrate himself back into society. 

Interpretation: Enter a person's world and operate inside it, under their rules, gently guiding them out of the hall of mirrors they have entered. 

Observance VI: Takeno Sho-o was a great tea master that always seemed to figure out what his guests wanted. 

Interpretation: Learn to manipulate the Courtier's mirror, for it will bring you great power. 


Observance VII: Yellow Kid Weil dressed up a bank to lure rich people into making huge fake deposits. 

Interpretation: The mirroring of reality offeres immense deceptive powers. In this particular game it is the first moment that counts the most. 

A warning: Beware of Mirrored Situations

Richard Wagner mirrored the footsteps of Lola Montez and got himself removed from Ludwig's good side for the same reasons. 






